### 3. Controversy & Counter-Intuitive Points

**Conflict 1: The GPU Utilization Trap**
> **Industry says:** "Monitor your GPU utilization to understand AI infrastructure health."
> **Aman says:** "Beyond a certain GPU memory threshold, inference performance is determined by optimization choices — batching strategy, KV cache utilization, attention mechanism tuning — not raw GPU metrics. GPU utilization being high doesn't mean your system is performing well; it may mean your engine is inefficient."
>
> Evidence: *"Beyond a certain limit GPU memories are not the bottleneck to the inference performance, it's more of the optimizations that you do, how much your KV cache is being utilized, how much new tokens are generated."*
>
> Shareable potential: **High** — this is the clearest violation of the default assumption.

---

**Conflict 2: You Cannot Debug a Wrong LLM Response**
> **Industry says (implicitly):** "Observability means you can trace any problem back to its root cause."
> **Vishal says:** "With traditional software, you can find the line of code that caused an exception. With an LLM, you're sending a request and getting a response — there is no equivalent of a stack trace for a wrong answer. This is a fundamental, unsolved problem in AI observability, and no one in the industry has a complete answer today."
>
> Evidence: *"How do you know and how do you debug, for lack of a better word, what caused a certain exception or a certain response to be not right and wrong? I don't think there is a right answer and 100% clear answer today as an industry. That will continue to evolve."*
>
> Shareable potential: **High** — most observability content implies the problem is solvable with the right tools. Vishal is saying the category of problem is fundamentally different in kind.

---

**Conflict 3: Networking Is Not Just a Training Problem**
> **Common assumption:** "High-speed networking (InfiniBand, RDMA) matters for distributed training, but not for inference."
> **Aman says:** "Inference with tensor parallelism or data parallelism across multiple nodes requires the same high-speed cross-node communication as training. If you're not measuring your InfiniBand bandwidth and RDMA throughput during inference, your performance specs exist only on paper."
>
> Evidence: *"If you're using parallelism techniques, you need to make sure that your inference engines are basically communicating at those speeds... there's no value if it is only on paper."*
>
> Shareable potential: **Medium** — this is only relevant to teams running multi-node inference, but it is a genuine surprise for most readers.

---

**Conflict 4: Don't Build a Comprehensive Observability Stack**
> **Industry says:** "Comprehensive, full-stack observability is always better."
